1 NAME Joseph /Fox/
2 GIVN Joseph
2 _MARNM Fox
1 SEX M
1 BAPM
2 ADDR
3 CITY Independence
3 STAE Kansas
3 CTRY United States
1 BIRT
2 DATE 24 DEC 1863
2 PLAC Mistoff, Bohemia, Germany, Austria
2 ADDR
3 CTRY Germany
1 DEAT
2 DATE 13 JUL 1928
2 ADDR
3 STAE Nebraska
3 CTRY United States
1 BURI
2 DATE 15 JUL 1928
2 PLAC Highland Cemetery
2 ADDR
3 CITY Bertrand
3 STAE Nebraska
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} When he came over changed his last name from Fuches to Fox. The family story goes that 2 brothers married 2 sister from the same village they came from in Bohemia. Info from: `Smithfield Cenntennial Book', old obit
2 CONC uary clipping from a newspaper, visiting with his sons, a large family picture, and a copy of the family history out of the `Gosper County Book', `Death Certificate' born Dec. 24, 1863, died July 13, 1928, aged 65 y, 6 mo, 18 days
2 CONC , and `viewed gravesite at Highland Cemetery, Phelps Co., Nebraska `1900 US Census' has him born in Austra. Immigrated in 1885, 15 years in US, not naturalized, farmer, literate. `Marriage License Record' Joseph & Mary Seaman were
2 CONC  married July 20, 1893 in Gosper County by Judge... He came from Mistof, Bohemeha Conupendorf, Germany (now Czechoslovakia) when he was 19 years old to avoid serving in the army. He spent 1 year in Waterloo, Wisconsin (by St. Paul
2 CONC ). Then he came to Bertrand, Nebraska. The town was just staked out. He worked as a farmhand on the John Glenn Ranch. He was very pleased with the country and decided this was where he wanted to make his home. Joseph claimed to be
2 CONC  the first man to sleep in Bertrand. One night on his way home from work, he got lost and stumbled on some stakes and couldn't find his way out so he just laid down and went to sleep. The next morning he realized he was inside the
2 CONC  soon-to-be town of Bertrand. He met his wife in Wisconsin and asked her to come down. In 1893 He married Mary Simon at Bertrand. In 1894 they bought a farm with a sod house on it, 1 1/4 mile west of Bertrand, later known as the K
2 CONC enneth Maaske farm and started farming. In 1904 they purchased the present 320 acre Fox farm from William & Margaret Hanson. Mr. Hanson had purchased the land from the Union Pacific Railroad in 1893, which was Sec. 25, T 8, R 22, 
2 CONC 5 miles Northeast of Elwood, Nebraska. Moved there in 1905. Joe Fox was a hardworking man and a good manager. with the help of his sons and a hard working wife, he was able to acquire 720 acres more of land. 400 acres was south of
2 CONC  Smithfield and is now owned by Dale Fox-grandson. 160 acres was north of Smithfield and is now owned by Robert Hanson-grandson. Another 160 acres was owned by Johnnie Fox & farmed by Francis Fox, later Steve & Mike Guardine, gr-g
2 CONC randsons until sold. He fed many cattle and drove them to the shipping yards in Smithfield, operated by John Glenn. From there they were shipped to Omaha by train. He also raised lots of hogs that were hauled to Smithfield by team
2 CONC  & wagon where he sold them to John Glenn. He also milked cows. He raised corn, wheat, oats, alfalfa and prairie hay that was farmed with two outfits of machinery pulled by six head of horses on each outfit. After his death, their
2 CONC  first tractor was purchased that took the place of the horses. He helped build the St. John's Catholic church in Smithfield. Also one of 1st church board members and donated a stove. His children were all baptized there. He passe
2 CONC d away at the age of 64 at his home 8 miles NE of Elwood. Sandstrom Funeral Home of Bertrand. Funeral services were held Sunday forenoon at the St. John's Catholic Church in Smithfield, conducted by Rev. Gearhearstein of Holdrege.
2 CONC  Resident of Gosper county 24 years. He was 5'7" with light brown hair-went bald, and medium build. He spoke German then learned English. He had 6 sons and 2 daughters.
2 CONT
