1 OCCU 5.6.1937: Expert in aviation ("Hindenburg" - catastrophy Lakehurst, New Jersey 
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://nationalaviation.org/loening-grover/
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grover_Loening
2 CONT
2 CONT Grover Cleveland Loening (September 12, 1888 – February 29, 1976) was an American aircraft manufacturer.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Loening was born in Bremen, German Empire, on September 12, 1888 while his American-born father was stationed there as U.S. Consul. He graduated from Columbia University in New York City, where he was awarded the first-ever degree
2 CONC  in Aeronautical Engineering.
2 CONT
2 CONT Following graduation, he joined the Queen Aeroplane Company in New York, managed the Wright Company factory in Dayton, Ohio for Orville Wright in 1913 and 1914, published a book, Military Airplanes, and became Vice President of th
2 CONC e Sturtevant Aeroplane Company and Chief engineer for the Army in San Diego. In 1917 he formed the Loening Aeronautical Engineering Corporation; after it merged with Keystone Aircraft in 1928, he formed the Grover Loening Aircraft
2 CONC  Company. His work on the Loening Flying Yacht won the 1921 Collier Trophy. His notoriety increasing in 1927, Loening dated Elizabeth Nast, a Vogue model twenty years his junior with an affinity for flying. Loening would test fly 
2 CONC his own aircraft. Among his employees were Leroy Grumman, William T. Schwendler, and Jake Swirbul who would go on to form Grumman. The company eventually closed in 1933. During World War II he was chief consultant to the War Produ
2 CONC ction Board, NACA, and Grumman.
2 CONT
2 CONT Asked how to say his name, he told The Literary Digest: "The correct American pronunciation, used by me and universally in aviation, is one that ignores the e or the umlaut suggestion; viz., the low is pronounced as low to rhyme w
2 CONC ith doe, and accenting the first syllable—lo'ning." (Charles Earle Funk, What's the Name, Please?, Funk & Wagnalls, 1936.)
2 CONT
2 CONT He is a 1976 recipient of the Langley Gold Medal from the Smithsonian Institution.
2 CONT
2 CONT He died on February 29, 1976 in Coconut Grove, Florida.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography
2 CONT
2 CONT Loening, Grover. Our Wings Grow Faster: In These Personal Episodes of a Lifetime in Aviation May Be Found an Historical and Pictorial Record Showing How We So Quickly Stepped Into This Air Age - and Through What Kind of Difficulti
2 CONC es and Developments We Had to Pass to Get There. Garden City, New York: Doubleday, Doran & Co., 1935.
2 CONT
2 CONT Loening, Grover. Takeoff Into Greatness: How American Aviation Grew So Big So Fast. New York: G.P. Putnam's Sons, 1968.
2 CONT
2 CONT Loening, Grover. Conquering Wing. Philadelphia: Chilton Book Company, 1970.
2 CONT
2 CONT Loening, Grover. Amphibian; The Story of the Loening Biplane. Greenwich, CT: New York Graphic Society, 1973. isbn: 0-8212-0522-6
